      "Not every piece of teak deserves a full restoration. A bench that has been out for six winters, silvered in patches, and stained where a planter sat on it is a candidate for a different approach: stop trying to recover the original colour and give it a consistent one instead. Cabot's Australian Timber Oil was formulated for exactly the dense tropical species — jarrah, merbau, and by extension teak — that shrug off ordinary deck stains, and its long-oil alkyd resin penetrates hard, tight grain rather than sitting on it. The iron oxide pigments do the heavy lifting on longevity. Because they are mineral rather than dye-based, they hold their colour across two and often three seasons outdoors, which is longer than any clear or lightly-tinted product here can manage. On a weathered teak bench in our test group, one coat evened out patchy silvering that a natural-tone sealer had left looking blotchy, and the second coat brought it to something that genuinely read as a finished piece of furniture rather than a rescue job. Two caveats matter. It is unmistakably a stain, so anyone chasing the pale, matte look of freshly brightened teak should choose something else. And the dry time is long — plan on a full day before light handling and closer to two before anyone sits on it — which makes it a job for a settled dry spell rather than a Saturday morning.",
  },
  {
    id: "star-brite-tropical-teak-classic",
    name: "Star brite Tropical Teak Sealer Classic",
