      "The Tropical Teak Sealer Classic is the budget entry in Star brite's teak line, and it is a more sensible purchase than its price suggests. Unlike the Golden Teak Oil, it carries actual pigment, which is what physically blocks ultraviolet light and slows the greying process; unlike the premium pigmented sealers, it is thin and cheap enough that recoating feels trivial rather than like a decision. On our test pieces it delivered roughly four to five months of good colour on horizontal surfaces in summer sun before wanting a refresh — meaningfully shorter than Semco, but at a fraction of the cost and with an application that takes minutes. Wipe it on with a rag, keep it moving so it does not pool, and wipe back anything that has not absorbed. Where it disappoints is depth. Set next to a piece treated with the Golden Teak Oil, the Classic looks flatter and slightly more uniform, which reads as clean rather than rich. That is a fair description of the whole product: it protects teak competently and cheaply without ever making it look spectacular. For a large set of garden furniture that gets used hard, has children and drinks on it, and is not the focal point of anything, that is exactly the right set of priorities. For a cockpit table you want people to notice, spend more.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Teak Sealer", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-teak-sealer/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best teak sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most teak furniture, Semco Teak Sealer is the best overall choice: a single thin coat, honest colour retention through a season, and a recoat that needs a rinse rather than stripping. For boat trim exposed to salt and standing water, TotalBoat Danish Teak Sealer is the better formulation, and for a teak deck where recoating everything twice a year is unrealistic, Messmer's UV Plus holds up longest.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you have to seal teak furniture?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Teak contains natural oils and silica that make it rot-resistant without any finish at all, and unsealed teak weathers to a silver-grey patina without losing structural integrity. Sealing is purely about appearance: it preserves the honey-brown colour that greys within about six to nine months of sun exposure. If you like the silver look, leaving teak bare is a legitimate choice.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should teak sealer be reapplied?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most pigmented teak sealers need recoating every six to twelve months, with horizontal surfaces like table tops and seat slats wearing roughly twice as fast as vertical legs and rails. Clear oils fade faster, often at three to four months in full sun. Pigment-heavy hardwood finishes such as Messmer's UV Plus or Cabot Australian Timber Oil can stretch to two seasons.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is teak sealer better than teak oil?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For outdoor teak, sealer is generally the better choice. Teak oil looks beautiful immediately but contains no pigment, so it does little to stop UV greying, and it feeds surface mildew in damp conditions. A pigmented sealer physically blocks ultraviolet light and holds colour several times longer. Teak oil still has a place on interior teak, where UV exposure is minimal.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you apply teak sealer over old sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, provided it is the same product and the surface is clean. Penetrating sealers wear away rather than building a film, so a wash, a rinse and a full dry is usually enough before recoating. Switching brands or moving from an oil to a sealer is different: strip and clean back to bare wood first, because residue from the old product blocks the new one from penetrating.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why did my teak turn grey again so quickly?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always one of three causes: a clear product with no pigment was used and offered no real UV defence; the wood was not cleaned and brightened before sealing, so the sealer sat on residue rather than penetrating; or the piece lives in unbroken direct sun, which shortens every interval. Horizontal surfaces in full sun are the first to go, and they are worth spot-recoating between full applications.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need to sand teak before sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually not. A two-part teak cleaner and brightener does the work chemically, opening the grain and restoring colour without removing wood. Sanding is worth it only where the grain has raised badly or a previous film finish is flaking, and then a light pass at 120 to 150 grit with the grain is enough. Heavy sanding removes teak's natural silica-rich surface and shortens the life of the piece.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is teak sealer safe on a boat deck you walk on?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Penetrating sealers are the safer category here because they leave no film on the surface, so the wood keeps its natural grip when wet. Varnishes and other film finishes become genuinely slippery underfoot on a wet deck or swim platform. Follow the label on ventilation and cure time, particularly if any of the work happens below deck.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
