        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="does-teak-need-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Does Teak Actually Need Sealing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Structurally, no. Teak's reputation is earned: the heartwood carries natural oils and a high silica content that make it resistant to rot, insects, and the freeze-thaw cycling that destroys most outdoor timber. Untreated teak benches sit in public parks for thirty years. Untreated teak decks on old boats outlive the people who laid them. If your only concern is whether the furniture will still be there next decade, you can stop reading and leave it bare.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason this page exists is colour. Ultraviolet light breaks down lignin — the compound that binds wood fibres together and carries most of teak's warm tone — at the surface. As the lignin degrades, the exposed cellulose scatters light differently, and the wood reads silver-grey. In full sun this begins within weeks and is usually complete within <strong>six to nine months</strong>. In partial shade it takes a year or more. Nothing about that process weakens the wood; it is a cosmetic change confined to a layer a fraction of a millimetre deep, which is why a good scrub with a two-part cleaner brings the colour straight back.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest framing of the decision is this: sealing teak is a maintenance commitment you take on for aesthetic reasons. A sealed piece looks warm and cared-for, and asks for an hour or two of attention once or twice a year, indefinitely. An unsealed piece looks silver and asks for nothing. Both are defensible. What is not defensible is sealing once, never recoating, and then being surprised three years later when the finish has worn away unevenly and the wood looks patchy — which is the single most common way teak ends up looking bad.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Sealing Actually Buys You</p>
