        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <div id="types-of-teak-finish" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Teak Sealer vs Teak Oil vs Varnish: What You Are Choosing Between
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three product categories get sold for teak, and they behave so differently that picking the wrong category matters far more than picking the wrong brand within a category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pigmented Penetrating Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            These are thin, oil- or water-borne carriers loaded with fine pigment. They soak into the open grain and leave no measurable film on the surface. The pigment is the active ingredient: it physically blocks and scatters ultraviolet light before it can reach the lignin. Because there is no film, there is nothing to peel, and a worn area can be recoated without stripping anything. Semco, TeakGuard and Star brite's Tropical Teak line all sit here. This is the category most teak owners should be shopping in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Teak Oils</h3>
          <p>
            Teak oil is usually a blend of linseed or tung oil with solvents, sometimes with resins and UV absorbers added. It penetrates, darkens the wood, and produces the richest immediate colour of anything you can put on teak. What it mostly does not contain is pigment, so its UV defence is limited to whatever absorbers the maker has added — and absorbers are consumed as they work. In damp, shaded conditions the oil itself can also feed surface mildew, which is why teak that has been oiled and then left under a cover often comes out speckled black. We cover the trade-off in detail in our <a href="/teak-oil-vs-teak-sealer/">teak oil vs teak sealer comparison</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Varnishes and Film Finishes</h3>
          <p>
            Marine varnish builds a hard, glossy film on the surface, and on vertical brightwork that is protected from standing water it looks extraordinary. On outdoor furniture and anything horizontal it is a poor bet. The film expands and contracts at a different rate to the wood beneath, water gets under any breach, and the failure mode is flaking rather than gradual thinning — which means stripping back to bare wood and rebuilding six to eight coats. It also becomes slippery when wet, so it has no business on a step, a sole, or a swim platform.
          </p>

