        <div id="how-to-apply-teak-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply Teak Sealer Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The application itself is the easy part. Almost every disappointing teak finish traces back to what happened — or did not happen — before the sealer came out of the tin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Strip Anything That Is Already There</h3>
          <p>
            If the piece has been oiled or sealed before with a different product, that residue has to go. Penetrating finishes work by getting into open grain, and old oil blocks the pores. A dedicated teak stripper or the first part of a two-part cleaner will lift it; work it with a soft brush, always along the grain, never across it. Teak's surface is soft between the harder grain lines, and a stiff brush used carelessly digs the softer wood out and leaves a ridged, furry texture that no finish will hide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Clean and Brighten</h3>
          <p>
            Two-part teak cleaners exist because the job needs two chemistries. Part one is alkaline and removes grey, dirt and biological growth; part two is acidic, neutralises the first, and restores the wood's natural colour. Skipping the second part leaves the wood alkaline and slightly darkened, and the sealer will look muddy over it. Rinse thoroughly between steps and afterwards. Avoid chlorine bleach entirely — it strips teak's natural oils, whitens the wood in a way that looks bleached rather than clean, and can damage adjacent fasteners and fabric.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Dry Properly</h3>
          <p>
            Teak feels dry on the surface long before it is dry in the grain. Give it a minimum of <strong>24 hours</strong> after cleaning, and 48 if the weather is cool or humid. Sealing over damp wood traps moisture, and the finish will look cloudy or lift in patches within weeks. This is the step people skip when the weekend is running out, and it is the most expensive one to get wrong.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Apply Thin, and Wipe Back</h3>
          <p>
            Thin coats are not a suggestion. Teak's tight, oily grain accepts far less product than the coverage figure on the tin implies, and anything the wood does not absorb stays on the surface, dries tacky, and collects dust for weeks. Work a section at a time with a brush, foam applicator or rag, give it ten to fifteen minutes, then wipe back every trace that has not soaked in. If you are applying a second coat, follow the label — some products want the second coat while the first is still slightly wet, others want a full cure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Keep It Off the Wet Grass and Out of the Rain</h3>
          <p>
            Most teak sealers want a dry period of at least a few hours and preferably overnight. Check the forecast for the following day as well as the current one, and be realistic about dew: furniture sealed at five in the afternoon on a cool September evening will still be curing when the moisture settles. Morning application on a settled, dry day is worth waiting for.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Do the Undersides Once</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Nobody sees the underside of a teak table, and nobody needs to seal it every year — but sealing it <strong>once</strong>, at the first application, evens out how the piece takes up and releases moisture. Furniture sealed only on top can cup slightly over several seasons as the two faces move at different rates. One coat underneath at the start removes the problem for the life of the piece.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

