          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Furniture vs Decking vs Boat Trim: Different Jobs, Different Products
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garden Furniture</h3>
          <p>
            Furniture is the most forgiving case. Surfaces are small, everything is reachable, and a full recoat is an evening's work rather than a project. That argues for a product optimised for looks and ease rather than for maximum interval — Semco for a natural, well-kept appearance, or Star brite's Golden Teak Oil if you want the richest colour and do not mind refreshing it more often. The horizontal surfaces will always wear first; there is no shame in giving the table top and seat slats a mid-season touch-up while leaving the legs alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Teak Decking</h3>
          <p>
            Decking flips the priorities. The area is large, the work is on your knees, and everything is horizontal and walked on, which is the fastest-wearing combination there is. Here the recoat interval is the specification that matters, and a pigment-heavy hardwood finish like Messmer's UV Plus earns its price by turning a twice-yearly job into a yearly one. Accept that it will look like a finished deck rather than raw teak — over that area, consistency reads better than authenticity anyway. Our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a> covers the wider category if the deck is a mix of species.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Boat Trim and Brightwork</h3>
          <p>
            Marine teak faces salt, constant rinsing, long spells under covers, and — on anything walked on — a genuine safety requirement for grip when wet. That rules out varnish on soles and platforms and argues for a marine-formulated penetrating sealer with mildew inhibitors, which is exactly what the TotalBoat product is. Vertical brightwork such as handrails and coamings is the one place where varnish still makes sense, because water runs off rather than standing, and the payoff in appearance is real.
          </p>

