        <div id="maintenance-schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Realistic Maintenance Schedule
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealed teak piece that looks good in year five has been maintained on a rhythm, not rescued periodically. The rhythm is not demanding, but it does have to exist.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Every Few Weeks in Season: Rinse</h3>
          <p>
            Pollen, dust, bird droppings and spilled food all hold moisture against the surface and give mildew somewhere to start. A hose and a soft brush, along the grain, takes two minutes and prevents the staining that otherwise forces a full clean-and-reseal. Wipe up anything oily — sun cream and salad dressing are the two worst offenders on a teak table — before it has a chance to soak in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Twice a Year: Assess the Horizontals</h3>
          <p>
            Sprinkle water on the table top and the seat slats. If it beads, the sealer is still working. If it darkens the wood and soaks in within about thirty seconds, those surfaces want a coat. There is no need to treat the whole piece: recoating only the worn horizontal surfaces keeps the colour even and takes a fraction of the time. This is the single habit that separates teak that looks consistently good from teak that gets restored every few years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Annually: Clean, Brighten, Reseal</h3>
          <p>
            Once a year, ideally in spring before the strongest sun arrives, give the piece the full treatment: two-part clean, thorough rinse, a proper drying period, then a fresh coat everywhere. On a piece that has been kept up this is quick, because you are recoating over a surface that never fully failed rather than restoring bare grey wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Winter: Cover, But Let It Breathe</h3>
          <p>
            A sealed teak piece does not need to come indoors, but a cover extends the finish considerably by keeping off the worst of the winter wet. The rule is airflow: a tight, unventilated cover traps condensation against the wood and produces exactly the black mildew speckling the cover was meant to prevent. Use a breathable cover, raise the furniture slightly off wet paving or grass, and check under it once or twice over the winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Teak Sealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Teak is forgiving about neglect and unforgiving about shortcuts. These are the five failures we see most often.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Without Cleaning First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating sealer has to reach open grain. Applied over grey, dirty, or previously oiled wood, it sits on the surface, dries unevenly, and lifts within a season. Every hour spent on cleaning and brightening buys more finish life than any upgrade in product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Clear Product to Stop Greying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pigment is what blocks ultraviolet light. A completely clear oil or sealer repels water beautifully and lets the wood grey underneath it anyway. If holding the honey colour is the goal, some pigment is not optional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Scrubbing Across the Grain With a Stiff Brush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Teak has soft wood between hard grain lines. A stiff brush worked across the grain — or a pressure washer held close — erodes the soft bands and leaves a permanently ridged, splintery surface. Soft brush, along the grain, always.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flooding It On</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Teak absorbs far less than the coverage figure suggests. Excess product stays on the surface, cures tacky, and traps dirt for weeks. Thin coats, and wipe back anything not absorbed within fifteen minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching Products Without Stripping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Recoating the same sealer over itself is fine. Putting a water-based sealer over an old oil, or a pigmented sealer over a varnish remnant, is not — the new product cannot penetrate and will fail patchily. Changing category means going back to bare wood.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best teak sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most teak furniture, Semco Teak Sealer is the best overall choice — one thin coat, honest colour retention through a season, and a recoat that needs a rinse rather than stripping. For boat trim exposed to salt and standing water, TotalBoat Danish Teak Sealer is the better formulation. For a teak deck, where recoating everything twice a year is not realistic, Messmer's UV Plus for Hardwoods holds up longest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to seal teak furniture?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Teak's natural oils and silica make it rot-resistant with no finish at all, and unsealed teak weathers to a silver-grey patina without losing strength. Sealing is a purely cosmetic decision: it preserves the honey-brown colour that otherwise greys within six to nine months of sun exposure. Plenty of people prefer the silver look and leave it bare deliberately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should teak sealer be reapplied?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most pigmented teak sealers want recoating every six to twelve months, with horizontal surfaces wearing roughly twice as fast as vertical ones. Clear oils fade sooner — often three to four months in strong sun. Pigment-heavy hardwood finishes such as Messmer's UV Plus or Cabot Australian Timber Oil can stretch to two seasons. Use the water test rather than the calendar: if water soaks in within thirty seconds, it is time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is teak sealer better than teak oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Outdoors, yes. Teak oil gives the richest immediate colour but carries little or no pigment, so it does not stop UV greying for long, and in damp shaded conditions it can feed surface mildew. A pigmented sealer physically blocks ultraviolet light and holds colour several times longer. Teak oil still makes sense on interior teak, where UV exposure is minimal and the look is the whole point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply teak sealer over old sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is the same product and the surface is clean. Penetrating sealers wear away rather than building a film, so a wash, a rinse and a full dry is normally all the prep a recoat needs. Switching brands, or moving between an oil and a sealer, is a different matter — strip back to bare wood first, because residue from the old product blocks the new one from penetrating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my teak turn grey again so quickly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually one of three reasons: the product had no pigment and therefore no real UV defence; the wood was not cleaned and brightened first, so the sealer never penetrated; or the piece sits in unbroken direct sun, which shortens every interval. Horizontal surfaces always fail first, and spot-recoating them between full applications is the cheapest fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to sand teak before sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. A two-part teak cleaner and brightener opens the grain and restores colour chemically, without removing wood. Sanding is worth doing only where grain has raised badly or an old film finish is flaking, and a light pass at 120 to 150 grit along the grain is enough. Heavy sanding removes teak's silica-rich surface layer and shortens the life of the piece.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is teak sealer safe on a deck you walk on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating sealers are the right category for anything underfoot, because they leave no film and the wood keeps its natural grip when wet. Varnishes and other film finishes become genuinely slippery on a wet deck, sole or swim platform. Follow the label on ventilation and cure times, especially for any work in an enclosed space.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

