          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">best teak sealer</strong> for most people is <strong class="text-white">Semco</strong>: one thin coat, real colour retention, and a recoat that does not involve stripping anything. On a boat, <strong class="text-white">TotalBoat Danish Teak Sealer</strong> handles salt and mildew properly and stays safe underfoot. On teak decking, where recoat interval decides whether the job actually gets done, <strong class="text-white">Messmer's UV Plus</strong> is worth its price. And for teak that has already silvered unevenly, <strong class="text-white">Cabot Australian Timber Oil</strong> makes it look intentional again.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Whichever you choose, the finish is only ever as good as the preparation underneath it. Clean, brighten, let the wood dry longer than feels necessary, apply thin, and wipe back what does not soak in. Do that, keep to a rhythm of one full coat a year with a mid-season touch-up on the surfaces that take the sun, and teak will hold its colour indefinitely — which, since it was never going to rot anyway, is the only thing you were buying.
